<section id="open-source">
  <h2>Open-source &amp; upstream</h2>
  <p>
    I work mostly on the Linux Wi-Fi stack: over 20 commits merged across
    five projects since April 2026.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>wpa_supplicant (hostap):</strong> fixed four bugs I
      reproduced on Wi-Fi 7 access points: stale per-link scan entries and
      per-link key clearing in multi-link (MLO) handling, duplicated
      elements in the Association Request, and falling back from PMKSA
      caching when the AP deauthenticates. Two of them came with new hwsim
      regression tests, and all four shipped in wpa_supplicant 2.12.
      Later I added a Multi-Link element fuzzer to hostap&rsquo;s test
      suite. It found a one-byte over-read reachable before authentication
      from a crafted beacon (low severity: the read stops at the next
      byte), which I fixed in the same patch series (merged October 2026,
      after 2.12).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Linux kernel:</strong> an rtw89 fix for RF-calibration
      timeouts on USB adapters (mainline since 7.2, tested on four Realtek
      USB chipsets), and
      cfg80211 error messages that say why the BSS lookup for an
      authentication or association failed, with KUnit tests (mainline
      since 7.3).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>NetworkManager:</strong>
      <a href="https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/NetworkManager/NetworkManager/-/merge_requests/2405">MR
      !2405</a>, merged July 2026. NetworkManager reads the AP-MLD address
      from the Multi-Link element and, when every BSSID seen belongs to one
      Wi-Fi 7 access point, uses the long background-scan interval instead
      of the default five-minute one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>wireless-regdb:</strong> updates to the database that sets
      the legal Wi-Fi channels and power limits for each country. Mine
      covered South Africa, Hong Kong, Russia, South Korea, Ukraine and
      Togo, each checked against the primary source: the national
      regulator&rsquo;s published rules, or for Ukraine&rsquo;s 60 GHz
      entry, ETSI EN 302 567.
      Also a <code>Reviewed-by</code> on another contributor&rsquo;s
      Russia 6 GHz patch.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>morrownr/rtw89:</strong>
      <a href="https://github.com/morrownr/rtw89/pull/76">PR #76</a>,
      merged April 2026, backports the RF-calibration fix to the
      out-of-tree DKMS driver.
    </li>
  </ul>
